WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:01.733
- [Instructor] In this video,

00:00:01.733 --> 00:00:02.880
we're gonna talk about something called

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.780
kinetic molecular theory,
which sounds very fancy.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.520
But as we'll see in the next few seconds,

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.740
or the next few minutes,

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.850
it actually helps build our intuition

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:14.430
for what is actually going on with the gas

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.030
or at least an approximation

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.100
of what's going on with the gas.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.810
So first, let's think
about the types of things

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.080
that we know we can measure about a gas

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.130
at a macro level.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:26.650
Now, what do I mean at a macro?

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:29.750
I'm saying at a large scale,
at a scale that's much larger

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.780
than the scale of atoms or molecules.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.150
And we know the types of
things that we can measure.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.480
We can measure pressure.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:38.380
How do we do you do that?

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:41.410
Well, pressure is just
force per unit area.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:42.300
So, you can do this.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.640
There's various contraptions
you can use to measure pressure

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.920
depending what you're using it for.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.440
Force, you can measure with springs

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.120
and you can apply a certain
forces to certain square areas.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.520
But these are all ways that
you can measure pressure

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.130
and we can measure the pressure
of a gas in a container.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:01.940
You can measure volume of a container.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.420
That's actually pretty straightforward.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:04.960
You can imagine a container

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.060
that looks something
like this, it's volume.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:09.260
We know how to find the volume

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:10.750
of a rectangular prism like this,

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.250
or even if it was sphere or
some other type of figure.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.640
There's many ways of measuring the volume

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.110
without even being able to observe

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:20.320
or even know that things
like molecules exist.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.183
We know how to measure temperature,

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.170
and we can do that in different scales.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:29.210
Kelvin is what we use 'cause
it's more of an absolute scale,

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:30.693
but you can use literally thermometers

00:01:30.693 --> 00:01:31.970
to measure temperature.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:33.660
And once again, you
can measure temperature

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.320
without knowing anything
about atoms or molecules

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.180
or whether they even exist.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:43.180
And you can also measure
an amount of a substance.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.690
And in particular, we could say,

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.270
you can measure the number of moles.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.330
Now you might say don't moles
involve a certain number

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.710
of a molecule or an atom.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:57.130
Well, they do, but the notion
of a mole actually existed

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:00.380
even before we knew
exactly how many molecules,

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:02.860
how many particles made up a mole.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.480
It was just viewed as an amount

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.280
where people knew it must
be some number of particles,

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.360
but they didn't know exactly.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.740
So all of these things, we
can measure at a macro level.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.400
And we know that we can connect them all

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.310
with the ideal gas equation

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.920
that tells us that pressure times volume

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.640
is equal to the amount of
the gas we're dealing with.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.780
And this is, of course, we're
talking about an ideal gas

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:26.640
and in future videos,

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.630
we'll talk about how some gases
approach being an ideal gas

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.930
while some are less than ideal.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.010
But the amount we have
measures the number of moles.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.620
You have your ideal gas constant

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.650
that just helps us make
all the units work out

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.410
depending on our units
for everything else.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:45.700
And then you have your
temperature measured in Kelvin.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:49.190
And, scientists long before
we were actually able

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:52.830
to know about things like
atoms or even observe atoms

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.460
or molecules directly, or even indirectly,

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.850
they were able to
establish this relationship

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.190
using these macro measurements.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:03.380
But how do these macro
measurements and this relationship

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.800
actually make sense at a molecular level?

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:11.160
And that's what kinetic
molecular theory provides us.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.520
It says, imagine the gas is being made up

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.980
of a bunch of really,
really the small particles.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.470
Those are really the gas molecules.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:23.770
And their collective volume is very small

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:26.020
compared to the volume of the container.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:29.600
So, it's mostly empty space
between those particles.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:34.210
Now, the pressure is
caused by these particles

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:37.320
bouncing into the sides of the container.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:38.680
Because at any given moment,

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.920
you have enough particles
bouncing off the side

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.970
of any unit area that it's
providing a force per unit area.

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:47.610
It's providing a pressure.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.750
It assumes that those collisions

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.720
are what's known as elastic,

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.380
which we'll study in much more
detail in a physics course,

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.960
but it really says that your
kinetic energy is preserved.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.910
You might already be
familiar with the notion

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.730
that kinetic energy is equal to one half

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.550
times mass times velocity squared.

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:08.080
And so the kinetic energy
of these particles,

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:10.270
when they bounce off,
their mass doesn't change.

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:11.750
The mass of the particles still there.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.440
And we're saying that the
velocity is going to be preserved.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.380
So you have all of these
really small particles,

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.330
even their collective volume is small

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:20.060
compared to the volume of the container.

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:21.160
They're providing the pressure

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.930
by having these elastic collisions

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:24.530
with the side of the container.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:28.230
And temperature is related
to the average kinetic energy

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:29.070
of these particles.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.120
It would be proportional.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.340
The higher the temperature,

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.010
the higher average kinetic energy.

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:35.200
Now average kinetic
energy is really important

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.570
because some of these particles

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:39.860
might be moving faster than others.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.070
And of course, N, the number of moles,

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:43.970
tells us how many particles
we're dealing with.

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:47.440
We know that each mole has
Avogadro's number of particles.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:50.650
So, if you just multiply the
most times Avogadro's number,

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:52.640
you have the number of particles.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.790
And what's cool about
kinetic molecular theory,

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:55.820
I know it's built as a theory,

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:57.900
but this is fundamentally what chemists

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.600
and physicists visualize

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.850
when they imagine a gas in
a container of some kind.

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.150
And just to make it a
little bit more clear,

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:08.090
the axioms you could say of
kinetic molecular theory,

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:10.880
the assumptions of it,
I'll give them here.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.790
And it's important to realize
that these are assumptions

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:17.040
and the real world, we have
slight variation from it,

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.160
but these assumptions get us a long way

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:21.780
to explaining the behaviors of gases.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:22.850
So, we've already talked about it.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:25.610
Gas consists of particles
in constant random motion.

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:26.650
We've already talked about that.

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:28.790
They're bouncing off the
side of the container.

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:31.650
The combined volume of the
particles is negligible

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:36.020
compared to the total volume
in which the gas is contained.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:37.300
And that also matters

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.140
when you talk about
things like ideal gases,

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:41.360
because if it stops becoming negligible,

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.150
then you have to start
thinking about the repulsive

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:45.850
and attractive interactions,
a little bit more.

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:48.130
The particles exert no attractive

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:49.630
or repulsive forces on each other.

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:52.380
And that kind of builds into
the last point I just made,

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:54.020
which is if they did,

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:57.660
then we're getting closer to
being a less than ideal gas.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.610
And we'll talk about that in other videos.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.870
The collisions between the
particles are completely elastic.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:04.830
So, they preserve kinetic energy

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.470
and it's actually, they
would also preserve momentum.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.700
And that the average kinetic
energy of the particles

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:12.210
is proportional to the Kelvin temperature.

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:13.540
And we already talked about that,

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:14.910
that the macro variable,

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:16.750
the macro measurement of temperature

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.510
is giving us an indication,

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.350
it's proportional to the
average kinetic energy

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:22.773
of the particles.

